KG42.register("rules-469071a99d66bf8d4189f083",{"id":"44-blockchain_phev_trading::v141-c-R008","paper":"44-blockchain_phev_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R008","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local clearing price","process_stage":"pricing","market_meaning":"The local energy broker acting as auctioneer determines the final trading prices. Scope: The iterative double auction for localized P2P electricity trading among charging and discharging PHEVs in a hotspot.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function pricing.local under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R008-E001","source":"coordination.local_operator","target":"pricing.local","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R008-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E08"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"The iterative double auction for localized P2P electricity trading among charging and discharging PHEVs in a hotspot.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E08"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"x_b_ij_n","symbol":"b_{ij}^{n}","meaning":"Buying bid price submitted by charging PHEV CV_i^n to the auctioneer for discharging PHEV DV_j^n in LAG_n; it enters the auctioneer's payment function.","kind":"operational","domain":"b_{ij}^{n} \\ge 0, i \\in C, j \\in Z, n \\in \\iota","unit":null,"indices":["i","j","n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E08","v141-b-g02-E09","v141-b-g02-E14","v141-b-g02-E15"]},{"variable_id":"x_s_ji_n","symbol":"s_{ji}^{n}","meaning":"Selling bid price submitted by discharging PHEV DV_j^n to the auctioneer for charging PHEV CV_i^n in LAG_n; it enters the auctioneer's reward function.","kind":"operational","domain":"s_{ji}^{n} \\ge 0, i \\in C, j \\in Z, n \\in \\iota","unit":null,"indices":["i","j","n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E08","v141-b-g02-E10","v141-b-g02-E14","v141-b-g02-E15"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"theta_l1","symbol":"l_1","meaning":"Positive cost factor in the discharging-PHEV cost function and denominator of the auctioneer's reward rule (l_1 > 0).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E14"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_rmin_j","symbol":"r_j^{\\min}","meaning":"Minimum reward for discharging PHEV DV_j^n owing to participation in local electricity trading.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"USD","indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E14","v141-b-g02-E23"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local energy broker acting as auctioneer determines the final trading prices. Scope: The iterative double auction for localized P2P electricity trading among charging and discharging PHEVs in a hotspot.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E08"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The payment function of charging PHEV CV_i^n, as given by the auctioneer, is the sum over discharging PHEVs j of its buying bid prices b_{ij}^n (Equation (15)).","expression":"\\operatorname{pay}_i(B_i^n)=\\sum_{j=1}^{J} b_{ij}^{n}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_b_ij_n"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E14","v141-b-g02-E15"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The reward function of discharging PHEV DV_j^n, as given by the auctioneer, is the sum over charging PHEVs i of (s_{ji}^n)^2/(4l_1), plus a minimum reward r_j^{min} for participation in local electricity trading (Equation (16)).","expression":"\\operatorname{Rew}_j(S_j^n)=\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\frac{(s_{ji}^{n})^2}{4l_1}+r_j^{\\min}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_s_ji_n"],"parameter_refs":["theta_l1","theta_rmin_j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E14","v141-b-g02-E15"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"supporting","statement":"The proposed auction is weakly budget balanced: the auctioneer does not suffer a loss, and the difference between charging-PHEV payment and discharging-PHEV reward is the auctioneer's benefit. The source does not print an aggregate inequality, and the numerical example reports averages over unequal participant counts, so no summation inequality is asserted here.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C005","kind":"logical","role":"supporting","statement":"Theorem 1 states that the pricing rules based on (15) and (16) make the optimal buying price and optimal selling price satisfy the bid-alignment conditions (13) and (14), respectively.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"KANG et al.: ENABLING LOCALIZED PEER-TO-PEER ELECTRICITY TRADING AMONG electricity, which is useful ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this section, an iterative double auction mechanism is proposed to elicit hidden information about PHEVs to energy brokers in order to maximize social welfare. In a hotspot, an energy broker acts as an auctioneer to perform an iterative double auction according to buying prices from charging PHEVs and selling prices from the discharging ones. In this way, the auctioneer determines the final trading prices and the amount of traded electricity, which is useful to avoid directly revealing private information of PHEVs during electricity trading. More specifically, each charging PHEV CV_i submits a bid price b_{ij} ≥ 0 for each discharging PHEV DV_j to the auctioneer. And each discharging PHEV DV_j also submits a bid price s_{ji} ≥ 0 for each charging PHEV CV_i to the auctioneer. After receiving these bid prices, the auctioneer solves an optimal energy allocation problem based on the bids from PHEVs, and thus allocates electricity for every PHEV to achieve effective market equilibrium [17]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3158","section":"IV. PROBLEM DEFINITION FOR ELECTRICITY TRADING","anchor_text":"where η is average charging efﬁciency from discharging PHEVs n is the charging willingness of CV in ","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the discharging PHEVs, d_{ji}^n is the amount of electricity supply from discharging PHEV DV_j^n to CV_i^n in LAGn. The electricity supply vector of DV_j^n is D_j^n ≜ {d_{ji}^n | i ∈ R}. In LAGn, the total electricity supply of all discharging PHEVs is D^n ≜ {D_j^n | j ∈ Z}. The maximum of electricity supply for a discharging PHEV in LAGn is D_j^{n,max}. In LAGn, the cost function of DV_j^n is L_j(D_j^n)=l_1 ∑_{i=1}^{I}(d_{ji}^n)^2 + l_2 ∑_{i=1}^{I}d_{ji}^n (2) where l_1 and l_2 are cost factors. Here, l_1 > 0."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"A. Entities in the Double Auction Mechanism (11 1) Charging PHEVs: The bid price vector of CVi for b","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"1) Charging PHEVs: The bid price vector of CV_i for buying energy in LAGn is B_i^n ≜ {b_{ij}^n | j ∈ Z}. All bid prices of charging PHEVs in LAGn are denoted as B^n ≜ {B_i^n | i ∈ C}. CV_i needs to solve the following optimal electricity buying problem (EB, buyer) to determine its optimal bid price: EB: max_{B_i^n} [ U_i(C_i^n) − pay_i(B_i^n) ] (8) where pay_i(B_i^n) is the payment function of CV_i given by the auctioneer."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"j DVj solves an optimal electricity selling problem (ES, seller) to determine its optimal bid price ","equation_id":"(9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"2) Discharging PHEVs: The bid price vector of DV_j for selling electricity in LAGn is denoted as S_j^n ≜ {s_{ji}^n | i ∈ C}. The bid price matrix of selling electricity is S^n ≜ {S_j^n | j ∈ Z}. DV_j solves an optimal electricity selling problem (ES, seller) to determine its optimal bid price as follows: ES: max_{S_j^n} [ Rew_j(S_j^n) − L_j(D_j^n) ] (9) where Rew_j(S_j^n) is a reward function of DV_j given by the auctioneer."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"J r) payi(Bni ) = bnij, (15) j 9) I (snji)2 Rewj(Snj ) = + rminj . (16) 4l1 i he For stimulation, rm","equation_id":"(15),(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"B. Pricing Rules The payment function of CV_i and the reward function of DV_j are, respectively, expressed as pay_i(B_i^n)=∑_{j=1}^{J}b_{ij}^n, (15) Rew_j(S_j^n)=∑_{i=1}^{I}\\frac{(s_{ji}^n)^2}{4l_1}+r_j^{\\min}. (16) For stimulation, r_j^{\\min} is a minimum reward for a discharging PHEV owing to the participation in local electricity trading."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"J r) payi(Bni ) = bnij, (15) j 9) I (snji)2 Rewj(Snj ) = + rminj . (16) 4l1 i he For stimulation, rm","equation_id":"(17)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: The pricing rules based on (15) and (16) make the optimal buying price and optimal selling price satisfy (13) and (14), respectively. Proof: For CV_i, the optimal buying price satisfies the following condition from (8) ∂U_i(C_i^n)/∂b_{ij}^n − ∂pay_i(B_i^n)/∂b_{ij}^n = 0. (17)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"∂Ui (Cni ) ∂Ui (Cni ) ∂cnij 0) According to (15), we obtain, ∂bnij = ∂cnij ∂bnij = ∂payi (Bni ) = 1.","equation_id":"(18),(19)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"According to (15), we obtain, ∂U_i(C_i^n)/∂b_{ij}^n = ∂U_i(C_i^n)/∂c_{ij}^n * ∂c_{ij}^n/∂b_{ij}^n = ∂pay_i(B_i^n)/∂b_{ij}^n = 1. Hence, b_{ij}^n = ∂U_i(C_i^n)/∂c_{ij}^n c_{ij}^n = \\frac{\\eta\\tau c_{ij}^n}{\\left(\\eta\\sum_{j=1}^{J}c_{ij}^n-c_i^{n,\\min}+1\\right)STO_i^n}. (18) Here, (18) and (13) are the same. For DV_j, the optimal buying price satisfies the following condition from (9) ∂Rew_j(S_j^n)/∂s_{ji}^n − ∂L_j(D_j^n)/∂s_{ji}^n = 0. (19)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":"3160","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"3160 IEEE TRANSA ∂Lj (Dnj ) ∂Lj (Dnj ) ∂dnji ne According to (16), we obtain, ∂snji = ∂dnji ∂snij = ","equation_id":"(20),(21)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"According to (16), we obtain, ∂L_j(D_j^n)/∂s_{ji}^n = ∂L_j(D_j^n)/∂d_{ji}^n * ∂d_{ji}^n/∂s_{ji}^n = ∂Rew_j(S_j^n)/∂s_{ji}^n = s_{ji}^n/(2l_1). Thus, we have ∂d_{ji}^n/∂s_{ji}^n = \\frac{s_{ji}^n}{2l_1(2l_1d_{ji}^n+l_2)}. (20) Based on (20), we consider that there exist linear correlations between s_{ji}^n and d_{ji}^n. We can easily obtain that s_{ji}^n = 2l_1d_{ji}^n+l_2. (21) Equations (21) and (14) are also the same. It is known that the pricing rules based on (15) and (16) can make optimal prices satisfy (13) and (14), respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":"3160","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"quency of restart. More details on the iterative double auction are given in Algorithm 1. According ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to (18) and (21), it is known that each PHEV will bid truthfully and maximize their own utilities through solving Problems EB and ES. Each PHEV’s utility is nonnegative. Our proposed auction is weakly budget balanced because the auctioneer does not suffer any loss while conducting the iterative double auction. Therefore, our proposed auction is truthful, individually rational, weakly budget balanced. This auction can achieve an optimal energy allocation solution with optimal social welfare in the energy market, where the auctioneer does not have complete information about PHEVs’ utility and cost functions for privacy protection [14], [17]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":"3161","section":"B. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"pp y g parking lots (see Fig. 4). We formulate electricity consumption tra behavior of PHEV as recor","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Therefore, we set the battery capacity of the PHEVs to 24 KWh. The minimum and maximum of electricity demand for charging PHEVs are [5, 10] KWh and [12, 18] KWh, respectively. The maximum of electricity supply for discharging PHEVs is [10, 20] KWh. The cost factors of cost function, i.e., l_1 and l_2 in (2), are set to 0.01 and 0.015, respectively. The average charging efficiency η is 0.8 and the average electricity transmission efficiency ρ is 0.9. The minimum reward for a discharging PHEV r_j^{min} randomly ranges from 1 to 2 dollars. The convergence threshold ε is 0.001. We take a parking lot with 35 charging PHEVs and 45 discharging in LAG1 as an example."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":9,"printed_page":"3162","section":"B. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"g charged electricity from all the discharging PHEV converges er- when the number of iterations is 2","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 7(b)","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Fig. 7(b) shows the convergence evolution of average payment for 35 charging PHEVs and average reward of 45 discharging PHEVs. The average payment of charging PHEVs for discharging PHEVs finally converges to 2.50 dollars. And the average reward of discharging PHEVs converges to 2.04 dollars after a few iterations. The difference between payment of charging PHEVs and reward of discharging PHEVs is the benefit of the auctioneer."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-matching"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E08","quote":"In this way, the auctioneer determines the final trading prices and the amount of traded electricity"}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"6c59b72425382715fdf5fffe1bd75100454abb8739b6000dc67b402c9914dc26","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The source gives explicit pricing rules (15)-(16) for the auctioneer's payment and reward functions, so the rule is formalized at the monetary-transfer level. The source does not define a single scalar local clearing price, and the full bid-alignment conditions (13)-(14) reference clearing/bidding quantities absent from R008's frozen V. B evidence remains unreviewed candidate support and has not been independently source-audited.","bindings":[{"object_id":"x_b_ij_n","node_ids":["pricing.local","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R008-E001"],"rationale":"The buying bid price is submitted to the local auctioneer and enters the local payment rule; the frozen edge links the local operator to local pricing."},{"object_id":"x_s_ji_n","node_ids":["pricing.local","coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R008-E001"],"rationale":"The selling bid price is submitted to the local auctioneer and enters the local reward rule; the frozen edge links the local operator to local pricing."},{"object_id":"theta_l1","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The parameter l_1 appears in the auctioneer's reward rule at pricing.local."},{"object_id":"theta_rmin_j","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The minimum participation reward appears in the auctioneer's reward rule at pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R008-C002","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R008-E001"],"rationale":"The local auctioneer applies the payment function through the local pricing relation; E001 is the frozen broker-to-pricing link."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R008-C003","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R008-E001"],"rationale":"The local auctioneer applies the reward function through the local pricing relation; E001 is the frozen broker-to-pricing link."}],"revision_requests":["C.1: R008 contains only coordination.local_operator and pricing.local. The pricing rules (15)-(16) reference bid prices submitted by PHEVs, and the bid-alignment theorem (13)-(14) references clearing quantities; add bidding.demand, bidding.supply, clearing.local, and/or settlement.payment with appropriate edges if the full pricing-to-bidding/clearing/settlement relation is to be represented.","B: The source does not define a scalar local clearing price p^n; it gives payment and reward functions. Clarify whether pricing.local should be represented by these monetary-transfer rules or by a separate scalar price variable.","B: The numerical example reports average payment 2.50 for 35 charging PHEVs and average reward 2.04 for 45 discharging PHEVs. This does not by itself establish an aggregate budget-balance inequality; the aggregation over participants remains unresolved."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
